# ~/.ssh/config — universal base, deployed on EVERY machine as a plain
# out-of-store symlink (modules/home/dotfiles.nix).
#
# Tiering (this file is tier 1 only):
#   1. universal      — this file: agent, multiplexing, keepalives, the seams
#   2. personal hosts — ~/.ssh/config.d/10-*.conf, op-rendered, personal identity
#                       only (they carry op:// values that must not be committed)
#   3. work hosts     — ~/.ssh/config.d/20-*.conf, owned by the external work
#                       overlay repo. Nothing employer-shaped belongs in here.
#
# This file therefore declares NO machine-specific Host stanza beyond the
# personal-GitHub alias below, which is identity, not infrastructure. It carries
# no op:// reference, so it needs no renderer and deploys identically everywhere.
#
# OrbStack injects `Include ~/.orbstack/ssh/config` and will NOT re-add it if
# removed, so it must stay first (before any Host block).

# OrbStack: 'orb' SSH host for Linux machines. Must stay before any Host blocks.
Include ~/.orbstack/ssh/config

# Extension seam: tier-2 and tier-3 fragments. MUST stay above every Host block
# — ssh_config is first-match-wins, so a fragment's specific Host stanzas can
# never win from below `Host *`. An empty glob is fine.
#
# `*.conf`, not `*`. Two reasons, both verified rather than assumed:
#   1. A single unparseable file in this directory does not degrade gracefully —
#      ssh exits with "terminating, 1 bad configuration options" and EVERY
#      connection fails, including the one you would use to fix it. Narrowing the
#      glob shrinks what can brick ssh to files a fragment author named .conf.
#   2. `*` matches editor leftovers like `10-homelab.conf~`. Those are read (they
#      lose on first-match to the real file, so they are silently ineffective
#      rather than wrong) — but a stale one that sorts FIRST would win. `*.conf`
#      excludes them. It matches the tmux seam's existing `*.conf` convention.
# Dotfiles are not matched by either form, so op-render's `.op-render.XXXXXX`
# tmpfile and the managed `.keep` marker are both invisible here.
#
# Fragments must be written atomically (render to a tmpfile, then rename). A
# partially-written .conf is indistinguishable from a malformed one.
Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*.conf

# Personal GitHub identity, by explicit alias.
#
# On a machine where plain `github.com` is bound to some other identity (a work
# overlay may claim it in a tier-3 fragment, which wins by being included
# above), this alias is the way to reach GitHub as the repo owner — e.g. to push
# this very repo.
#
# IdentitiesOnly stops the agent from walking every loaded key and
# authenticating as whoever answers first. Note the consequence: with
# IdentitiesOnly and a selector file that does not exist, ssh has NO identity to
# offer and this alias simply fails — it does not silently fall back to the
# agent's other keys. So the alias is inert on a machine lacking
# ~/.ssh/github-dotfiles.pub rather than equivalent to the default. That is the
# intended failure mode (explicit over ambiguous), but it does mean `ssh
# github-dotfiles` is only usable where that selector is present.
Host github-dotfiles
    HostName github.com
    User git
    IdentitiesOnly yes
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/github-dotfiles.pub

Host *
    # Survive laptop sleep / NAT idle: probe every 30s, give up after 3 misses.
    ServerAliveInterval 30
    ServerAliveCountMax 3
    # Multiplex repeat connections for instant reuse. %C hashes the connection
    # tuple, dodging macOS's 104-char unix-socket path limit.
    ControlMaster auto
    ControlPath ~/.ssh/cm-%C
    ControlPersist 10m
    # 1Password SSH agent — the single agent on every machine, personal and
    # work alike. Keys never live on disk; the .pub files are selectors only.
    #
    # Deliberately NOT carried over from the pre-nix hand-written config:
    # AddKeysToAgent / UseKeychain. Both concern adding on-disk private keys and
    # their passphrases to an agent/keychain. There are no on-disk private keys
    # here — only .pub selectors resolved through 1Password — so they never fire.
    # Omitting them keeps this file behaviorally identical to the template
    # personal machines run today.
    IdentityAgent "~/Library/Group Containers/2BUA8C4S2C.com.1password/t/agent.sock"
